From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anton Palgunov <toxblh@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:51:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 63/99] v8: e2k: overflow of a checked addition or subtraction
 from one compare

A branch on the overflow of Int64AddWithOverflow or Int64SubWithOverflow
made a flag in a register first: add, compare, merge 0/1, compare with
zero, branch - six wide commands on the Smi path of every arithmetic
bytecode handler and *_Baseline builtin. s = a + b overflowed when s - a
does (s - a is then b plus or minus 2^64), d = a - b when a - d does, and
cmpodb tells the overflow of a subtraction: the instruction is now the
plain operation and the branch one compare of the result with the left
operand, which the selector keeps out of the result register. The same in
32 bits with cmposb on the 64-bit sum of the sign-extended operands, in
place of add, sign extension and compare. A branch taken on overflow is a
rare one: the compare and the branch are one command.

On a stand the check costs 5 cycles the old way, 2 with ct and none as a
rare branch (lab, ideas/88-smi-ovf).
